---

Each file in `.hdr/decisions/` is one decision and holds that decision's whole
history, oldest entry first. Entries are only ever appended. Nothing in a
decision's past is edited or removed.

# One decision, one log

```json
{
  "schema_version": 2,
  "id": "HDR-003",
  "history": [
    {
      "date": "2026-10-21",
      "title": "Agents may not use shell tools",
      "rationale": "Shell commands can change anything, including the decision log.",
      "check": { "kind": "tool_denied", "tools": ["Bash"] }
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-11-02",
      "title": "Shell is allowed; the decision log is protected instead",
      "rationale": "The ban blocked builds and tests; the log now guards itself.",
      "check": { "kind": "path_exists", "path": ".hdr" }
    }
  ]
}
```

Reading the file top to bottom tells you what the decision was, how it changed
and why. Reading the last entry tells you what applies now.

# Current decisions

The **current decisions** are the last entry of every decision that has not
been retired. Only current decisions are checked. Earlier entries stay in the
file as history and are never evaluated.

| Decision | Latest entry | Current? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `HDR-001` | Decision records are append-only | yes |
| `HDR-002` | Register HDR checks for every lifecycle event | yes |
| `HDR-003` | Shell is allowed; the decision log is protected instead | yes, as revised |
| `HDR-004` | Retired: "Too blunt" | no |

`hdr validate` reports both numbers, for example
`PASS: 3 current decisions (4 decision files)`, and `hdr explain` shows each
decision's full history.

# Changing a decision

To change a decision, append an entry to its history. To stop enforcing it,
append a retirement:

```json
{ "date": "2026-12-01", "status": "retired", "rationale": "Replaced by HDR-007." }
```

A retired decision stays in the repository so its history remains readable.
The first entry of a history must be a decision, not a retirement, and dates are
optional `YYYY-MM-DD` strings.

To merge several decisions into one, retire each of them with a rationale that
names the replacement, and add the replacement as a new decision.

# Who may change what

| Change | Agent | Person |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Add a new decision with one entry | allowed | allowed |
| Append an entry to an existing decision | denied | allowed |
| Retire a decision | denied | allowed |
| Edit or remove an earlier entry | denied | flagged while a session runs |

When an agent writes a new decision, HDR validates it first. An invalid
decision, a duplicate ID, or a new file that already carries more than one
entry is denied with the reason, so the agent can fix its mistake or ask a
person.

># [Note]($block)
>HDR cannot tell who wrote a file. "Person" means a change made outside the
>agent's tool calls, such as in your editor. Shell commands an agent runs are not
>parsed; their effects are judged by the same history rules after the fact.

# How it is enforced

**Before a tool runs.** Each harness's file-editing tools are translated into
plain *edit* and *write* operations. An agent's edit or overwrite of an existing
decision file is denied; writing a new decision file is validated as above. This
includes `apply_patch` in Codex and opencode. See
[Harnesses]($link.page('harnesses')) for the tool mapping.

**Throughout the session.** When a session starts, HDR records every decision
file's digest and history in a per-session baseline under
`$XDG_STATE_HOME/hdr/sessions/`. On every later hook it compares the files with
that baseline:

- a new decision file is accepted and added to the baseline;
- a decision whose earlier entries are all unchanged, in order, with new entries
  after them, is accepted, and the new entries take effect immediately;
- anything else, such as a rewritten or removed entry or a deleted file, fails
  until the original history is restored.

This is what lets a person add or revise decisions during a session without
restarting it. The decision log is always checked this way, even without an
`append_only` decision; the built-in check is reported as `LOG-INTEGRITY`.

# Session baselines

- The baseline survives resume and compaction, which reuse the session ID.
- A new session starts a new baseline from whatever is on disk.
- Each harness keeps its own baselines, so sessions in different harnesses never
  collide.

# Rewriting history

You own the repository, so you can still rewrite a decision's history, for
example to fix a typo in an old entry. Do it between sessions: within a session,
any change that is not an append fails by design.

># [Caution]($block.attrs('warning'))
>Decision files are append-only, so their format must stay readable forever.
>Older single-record files (`"schema_version": 1`) are still read as one-entry
>histories, and replacing one with a `history` whose first entry matches the old
>record counts as an append. Their `supersedes` field, which retired other
>records, still works too.
